      "title": "[phys.org] Fire amoeba breaks complex-life heat tolerance record at 63°C",
      "content_html": "<img src=\"https://kagiproxy.com/img/lJX7ALbCMz-fS_PoSAEG6I2Qc3nx53FZVcLcGhp5-VvX-zuEsaK1a6RyGnieSd-PD96gO803skQyuMPHcwHNDcZ7EQIklB11i8UBLK7hOSW6xixT8HZiFabCfCyGCDHLNgmLQv1JeajvJe9yAxBd1AEyOF7sYVtC10DEooNMeCc17yyZfLLPjHHyuPot0DpdA9IY7Z8BBtwkcYOE-MQCIFHzScMnhS4\" style=\"width:100%;max-height:300px;object-fit:cover;\" />\n<p style=\"margin:0 0 0.75em;padding:5px 10px;background:#f0f4f8;border-left:3px solid #7b9fc4;border-radius:3px;font-size:0.82em;color:#444;line-height:1.8;\">🎨 🪶 🌲 🏔️ 🔭 <a href=\"https://zirnhelt.github.io/super-rss-feed/review.html?url=https%3A%2F%2Fphys.org%2Fnews%2F2026-09-hot-amoeba-tolerance-complex-life.html\" style=\"color:#7b9fc4;text-decoration:none;\">✏️</a></p>\n<img src=\"https://kagiproxy.com/img/lJX7ALbCMz-fS_PoSAEG6I2Qc3nx53FZVcLcGhp5-VvX-zuEsaK1a6RyGnieSd-PD96gO803skQyuMPHcwHNDcZ7EQIklB11i8UBLK7hOSW6xixT8HZiFabCfCyGCDHLNgmLQv1JeajvJe9yAxBd1AEyOF7sYVtC10DEooNMeCc17yyZfLLPjHHyuPot0DpdA9IY7Z8BBtwkcYOE-MQCIFHzScMnhS4\" style=\"width:100%;max-height:300px;object-fit:cover;\" />\nScientists have identified a new amoeba, Incendiamoeba cascadensis, that can eat, move and reproduce in water as hot as 63°C (145°F) at California’s Lassen Volcanic National Park, setting a record for the highest temperature known to support eukaryotic reproduction. The NASA-supported study, published in Cell on Sept. 22, found that the organism remains active at about 64°C and can recover after five minutes at 70°C, although exposure to 80°C proved fatal [phys.org#1][nasa.gov#1][livescience.com#1][sciencenews.org#1].\n\nThe finding challenges the previous 60°C upper limit for eukaryotic growth and reproduction, based on the heat sensitivity of proteins and other complex cellular structures. Researchers said the amoeba withstands the heat by changing shape and forming a protective outer layer; genetic analysis also identified genes associated with repairing heat-damaged proteins [nasa.gov#1][science.org#1][orf.at#1]. The work is relevant to astrobiology because scientists use extreme environments on Earth to assess where complex life might survive on Mars and other worlds [nasa.gov#1][npr.org#1].",
